+# Temporary-account provider linking design
+
+## Decision and scope
+
+The approved expansion adds a backend browser broker and a purpose-scoped,
+short-lived handoff credential for temporary-account provider linking. It is a
+design decision only; this change does not modify production code, providers,
+mobile clients, schema, or deployment configuration.
+
+The user approved this exact scope after the following proposal: “designing a
+temporary-account browser broker with a purpose-scoped short-lived handoff
+credential and native compatibility path, including expiry, migration and
+rollback. Permanent accounts keep the native flow. This approval would cover
+design only, with no production code changes.” The approved limits are:
+
+- permanent accounts retain the shipped native callback and existing grants;
+- the broker is initially enabled only for temporary accounts;
+- provider-specific behavior is behind adapters, with Polar as the first
+  provider and the contract applicable to every provider;
+- implementation, migration, deployment, and client release remain separate
+  future authorizations.
+
+This records the scope-expansion decision as **approved**. The expansion is a
+new browser return boundary, broker transaction, and handoff credential. It is
+owned by the provider-linking broker; the existing native flow remains owned by
+`mobile_oauth.py`.
+
+## Behavior
+
+Temporary account creation records `created_at`, `expires_at = created_at + 24
+hours`, and a lifecycle epoch. Expiry is authoritative account state. Every
+broker operation checks the account epoch and expiry at admission and again
+before storing a grant or publishing completion. Expiry rejects new work and
+invalidates outstanding transactions and handoffs.
+
+The temporary flow is:
+
+1. The app asks the broker to begin linking a provider for a temporary account.
+   The request binds account, provider, client flow, purpose
+   (`provider_link`), and account epoch. The broker persists a transaction with
+   a random transaction ID, PKCE challenge, redirect allowlist entry, expiry at
+   the earlier of account expiry or the transaction TTL, and status `pending`.
+2. The broker returns an authorization URL and an app-safe transaction handle.
+   No provider access grant or ordinary product session is returned in this
+   response.
+3. The provider redirects to the backend browser return endpoint. The endpoint
+   validates state, PKCE, provider, redirect registration, transaction status,
+   and transaction/account expiry before exchanging the authorization code.
+4. The broker stores the provider grant under the transaction's temporary
+   account and provider, using an idempotency key. The provider code and raw
+   callback parameters are discarded after exchange. A committed completion
+   record is created before any app redirect.
+5. The endpoint redirects to the registered app callback with a one-time,
+   opaque handoff credential. The credential contains no grant or personal
+   data in its value and is stored only as a hash with transaction ID, account
+   epoch, purpose, audience, issued time, expiry, and consumed state.
+6. The app submits the handoff to the broker's exchange operation. The broker
+   atomically checks audience, purpose, account epoch, expiry, transaction
+   status, and unused state, then marks it consumed and returns only the
+   provider-link result. It cannot establish a normal session, authorize a
+   different account or purpose, or be exchanged twice.
+
+The app may retry the exchange after a response timeout by presenting the same
+credential. A successful first exchange returns the same committed result by
+idempotency key during the bounded retry window; a second effect is never
+performed. Expired, consumed, mismatched, or unknown credentials return one
+uniform failure class without revealing account or provider state.
+
+Permanent accounts continue through `MobileOAuth.begin` and
+`MobileOAuth.complete`, using the permanent registry and existing grants. The
+broker must reject a permanent account in its temporary-account entry point.
+The compatibility adapter is a routing seam: it selects the broker flow for a
+temporary account and the existing native flow for a permanent account. It does
+not make the native callback registry authoritative for temporary accounts.
+
+## Contracts and invariants
+
+The broker exposes three contracts:
+
+- `begin(account, provider, client_redirect, pkce_challenge)` returns an
+  authorization URL and opaque transaction handle, or a typed rejection for
+  expired/unsupported account, provider, redirect, or client.
+- `browser_return(provider, code, state)` is provider-facing and returns only
+  an app redirect after a committed transaction or a generic failure page.
+- `exchange(handoff)` is app-facing and returns a provider-link result. It is
+  bound to the original account, provider, purpose, audience, epoch, and
+  transaction expiry.
+
+The transaction ID, handoff credential, account ID, provider, purpose, and
+timestamps are typed boundary values. Provider grants remain broker-owned
+secret material and are never placed in URLs, logs, browser state, or mobile
+responses. State-changing operations use compare-and-set on transaction status
+and lifecycle epoch. A callback arriving after expiry, deletion, account
+switch, or replay cannot write a grant or publish success.
+
+The browser endpoint is a provider callback boundary, not an authenticated
+product endpoint. State and PKCE establish the pending flow; they do not grant
+product authority. The exchange operation revalidates the full authorization
+context and fences the commit against account expiry/deletion.
+
+## Ownership map
+
+| Concept | Authoritative owner | Writers | Readers | Representations | Recovery or rebuild |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Account lifecycle and epoch | `accounts.py` account owner | account creation, expiry/deletion command | broker, native adapter | account record | Rebuildable from account record; deletion is explicit and increments/revokes epoch |
+| Broker transaction state | provider-linking broker | begin, callback, exchange/expiry worker | broker and operator diagnostics | durable transaction record | Resume by status and expiry; compare-and-set makes retry idempotent |
+| Provider grant link | provider-linking broker | successful callback exchange only | provider adapter and account-facing link query | encrypted grant record | Reconcile from committed transaction; never infer from callback absence |
+| Handoff authority | provider-linking broker | callback issuance, atomic exchange, expiry | app exchange endpoint | hashed credential plus metadata | No reconstruction after expiry; revoke by consume, epoch change, or deletion |
+| Native permanent link | `mobile_oauth.py` | existing native completion | existing native clients | permanent registry grant | Existing registry contract and rollback path |
+
+No client, provider, callback handler, or telemetry writer may become a second
+source of account lifecycle or grant-link truth.
+
+## Quality scenarios
+
+### Successful temporary link
+
+Stimulus: a non-expired temporary account starts Polar linking and completes the
+provider callback once.
+
+Operating condition: broker and provider are available; the app exchanges the
+handoff within its TTL.
+
+Expected response: exactly one grant link is committed to the temporary
+account, the app receives success, and no permanent registry entry is created.
+
+Measured bound: one provider code exchange and one successful grant commit per
+transaction, even if the browser return or app exchange is retried.
+
+Evidence source: broker contract tests with a synthetic provider.
+
+### Expiry during the flow
+
+Stimulus: the account reaches its 24-hour expiry after begin but before callback
+exchange or handoff exchange.
+
+Operating condition: callback, retry, or delayed worker arrives after expiry.
+
+Expected response: the operation is rejected; no grant, handoff success, cache,
+telemetry, or delayed retry recreates account state.
+
+Measured bound: zero post-expiry writes accepted for the expired epoch.
+
+Evidence source: focused check with expiry between each check/use boundary.
+
+### Handoff replay and substitution
+
+Stimulus: a handoff is replayed, altered, sent to another account, or sent to a
+different purpose/audience.
+
+Operating condition: attacker controls the browser return URL or app input.
+
+Expected response: exchange fails without revealing whether the transaction or
+account exists; a valid handoff can cause at most one effect.
+
+Measured bound: duplicate provider-link effects are zero; invalid exchange
+responses use one externally equivalent error class.
+
+Evidence source: security changed-path review and replay tests.
+
+### Permanent compatibility
+
+Stimulus: an existing permanent account uses each supported released native
+client and provider.
+
+Operating condition: broker flag is enabled for temporary accounts.
+
+Expected response: native callback resolution and existing grant storage are
+unchanged.
+
+Measured bound: no permanent callback is routed through the temporary broker in
+the initial rollout.
+
+Evidence source: existing native tests plus mixed-version client matrix.
+
+## Privacy, security, and operational requirements
+
+Account IDs, transaction metadata, provider identity, grant state, and
+handoff activity inherit the temporary account's sensitivity and 24-hour
+lifecycle unless repository authority documents a narrower retention. Logs and
+telemetry contain event type, outcome class, provider, and correlation ID only;
+they exclude credentials, authorization codes, raw callback parameters, grant
+contents, and full account identifiers. Expiry/deletion revokes future writers
+before records and derivatives are removed. Any external provider copy or
+already-disclosed data is reported as outside the broker's erasure boundary.
+
+The changed trust boundary requires a scoped security review covering callback
+confusion, state/PKCE binding, redirect validation, credential audience and
+purpose, replay, account substitution, expiry races, and grant disclosure. The
+changed lifecycle requires a scoped privacy review covering transaction/grant
+retention, logs, telemetry, caches, delayed callbacks, and deletion. This
+document is a design contract, not evidence that those reviews have passed.
+
+Operationally, the broker needs counters for begin/callback/exchange outcomes,
+expiry and replay rejections, provider exchange failures, and stale-epoch
+attempts. Correlation IDs must be non-secret. Alerts must not depend on a
+provider grant or handoff value. Cleanup is bounded and idempotent; it cannot
+block expiry or revocation behind optional telemetry.
+
+## Rollout, migration, and rollback
+
+The first release adds the broker behind `TEMP_PROVIDER_BROKER`, default off.
+While off, temporary accounts retain the current explicit unsupported result and
+permanent accounts retain native behavior. Enablement is account-class and
+provider scoped, with a kill switch that stops new broker begins while allowing
+already committed exchanges to finish only until their normal TTL.
+
+The compatibility adapter is additive during coexistence. Existing native
+clients require no new callback contract. New clients must understand the
+broker exchange result and generic failures; old clients remain on native flow
+for permanent accounts. Migration of permanent accounts or replacement of
+native OAuth is explicitly out of this design and requires a new approval.
+
+Rollback disables new temporary broker transactions, marks pending broker
+transactions unavailable after their TTL, and preserves permanent native
+operation. It does not delete committed provider links or silently move them
+into the permanent registry. A later re-enable may resume only non-expired,
+non-revoked transactions whose provider and client contract remain supported.
+Broker tables, adapter code, and telemetry are removable only after all
+transactions and handoffs have expired, committed links have an explicit owner,
+and operators have evidence that no supported client still depends on them.
+
+## Evidence classes
+
+- Synthetic/unit and contract tests: required for expiry, replay, idempotency,
+  provider error mapping, and permanent/native compatibility; pending until
+  implementation exists.
+- Host integration tests: required for callback routing, durable transaction
+  recovery, feature-flag rollback, and mixed client versions; pending.
+- Security and privacy changed-path reviews: required for the scoped boundaries;
+  pending and not performed by this design-only change.
+- Physical/mobile release proof and live-provider proof: not required for this
+  design artifact; required before claiming released-client or real-provider
+  compatibility.
